WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='128c15d4-625a-5892-b935-b947d55b6fd5' AND gt.achievement_id='e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'da269773-6b93-5c70-bbb1-de46971c7eb8','Sync-head offset','Sync-head offset is the time displacement that results from the physical spacing between a tape machine''s record head and its separate reproduce head. Because the tape reaches the reproduce head later than the record head, previously recorded tracks monitored from the reproduce head lag the record point; Sel-Sync (sync) playback reproduces those tracks from the record head itself to remove the offset so overdubs stay time-aligned.','On a tape machine the record head and play head sit apart. The tape reaches the play head a moment after the record head. So old tracks played from the play head arrive late while you record. That gap is the sync-head offset. Sync mode plays the old tracks from the record head to fix it, so overdubs line up.','Understanding the offset explains why overdubs must be monitored in sync mode: it stops new tracks being recorded out of time with existing ones by the head-to-head travel delay. Sel-Sync exists specifically to cancel this offset.','When overdubbing, an engineer switches the already-recorded tracks to sync (Sel-Sync) playback from the record head so the performer hears them in time and the new take aligns with them.','Heads & Sync',ARRAY['Sel-Sync','Sync-repro response','Reproduce head','Record head','Overdubbing','Head stack']::text[],ARRAY['Monitoring existing tracks from the reproduce head while overdubbing, so the new track is recorded late by the head-gap delay.','Assuming the offset is an electronic delay to dial in, when it is set by the physical distance between the heads and the tape speed.','Forgetting to switch tracks back to the reproduce head for mixdown after overdubbing in sync.']::text[],ARRAY['Overdubbing a vocal onto a multitrack tape and using sync mode to keep it in time.','Explaining why Sel-Sync was invented for multitrack recording.','Troubleshooting overdubs that sound rhythmically late against the existing tracks.']::text[],'e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='da269773-6b93-5c70-bbb1-de46971c7eb8' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Sync-head offset'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'da269773-6b93-5c70-bbb1-de46971c7eb8','e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='da269773-6b93-5c70-bbb1-de46971c7eb8' AND gt.achievement_id='e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '9c26d9bb-7c55-5aed-a219-79750096e0e8','Sync-repro response','Sync-repro response is the reproduce frequency response obtained when previously recorded tracks are played back through the record head in Sel-Sync (sync) mode rather than through the dedicated reproduce head. Because the record head''s gap and design are optimized for recording, its playback response, especially at high frequencies, differs from and is generally inferior to the reproduce head, so machines provide separate sync equalization and sync mode is used for monitoring during overdubs, not for mixdown.','In sync mode, old tracks play back through the record head, not the play head. But the record head is built for recording, not playing. So its sound is duller and loses some high frequencies. That difference is the sync-repro response. Engineers use it only to overdub in time, then switch to the play head for the final mix.','It describes the quality trade-off of Sel-Sync monitoring: sync playback keeps overdubs in time but sounds inferior to the reproduce head, so it is used for cueing rather than final sound. Separate sync equalization partly compensates for the record head''s non-ideal playback response.','A technician sets and checks sync equalization so monitoring in sync mode is usable, while reminding operators to switch all tracks to the reproduce head for mixdown to get full fidelity.','Heads & Sync',ARRAY['Sel-Sync','Sync-head offset','Reproduce head','Record head','Equalization','Overdubbing']::text[],ARRAY['Mixing down while still monitoring in sync mode, capturing the record head''s inferior response instead of the reproduce head''s.','Assuming sync and repro playback sound identical; the record head reproduces high frequencies less accurately.','Ignoring separate sync equalization during alignment, leaving sync monitoring more colored than necessary.']::text[],ARRAY['Explaining why an overdub cue mix sounds duller than the final playback.','Aligning sync-mode equalization on a multitrack machine.','Reminding an operator to leave sync mode before mixing down.']::text[],'e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='9c26d9bb-7c55-5aed-a219-79750096e0e8' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Sync-repro response'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
