SELECT '959812f0-7b62-5aae-b826-e2b42f3f0083','e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='959812f0-7b62-5aae-b826-e2b42f3f0083' AND gt.achievement_id='e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT '28012e70-dfe9-5afe-919d-b2bf4586b417','Tension arm','A tension arm (tension-sensing or compliance arm) is a pivoted, usually spring-loaded arm carrying a guide roller around which the tape passes, so that changes in tape tension move the arm. Its motion mechanically buffers the tape, smoothing tension surges and damping flutter, and, when coupled to a sensor, provides the feedback signal a reel servo uses to regulate tape tension.','A tension arm is a springy, pivoting arm with a small roller that the tape wraps around. When tape tension rises or falls, the arm swings. That movement smooths out sudden tugs so the tape runs evenly. On many machines a sensor reads the arm''s position. The reel motors then adjust to keep the tension steady.','It keeps tape tension steady and cushions sudden changes during starts, stops, and winding, which protects the tape and stabilizes speed and head contact. Where it feeds a servo, it closes the loop that actively regulates tension.','A technician verifies the tension arm moves freely, its spring is correctly set, and its sensor is calibrated, so the reel servo holds proper tension and the transport starts and stops smoothly.','Tape Transport',ARRAY['Take-up tension','Hold-back tension','Reel servo','Tape guide','Capstan','Wow and flutter']::text[],ARRAY['Bending or forcing a tension arm during cleaning, altering its spring calibration and the tension it sets.','Assuming the arm only guides tape, missing its roles in damping and tension feedback.','Ignoring a sticky or worn tension-arm pivot that causes tension swings and flutter.']::text[],ARRAY['Recalibrating a tension-arm sensor so the reel servo holds correct tape tension.','Damping start and stop tension surges that would otherwise stretch the tape.','Troubleshooting flutter traced to a sticking tension-arm pivot.']::text[],'e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='28012e70-dfe9-5afe-919d-b2bf4586b417' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Tension arm'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT '28012e70-dfe9-5afe-919d-b2bf4586b417','e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c',true
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary_topics gt WHERE gt.glossary_id='28012e70-dfe9-5afe-919d-b2bf4586b417' AND gt.achievement_id='e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c');
INSERT INTO glossary (id,term,definition,plain_english,purpose_function,practical_application,category,related_terms,common_mistakes,scenario_contexts,achievement_id)
SELECT 'c8309acc-2d56-5fa1-9cce-c72152f1f431','Thickness loss','Thickness loss (coating-thickness loss) is the reduction in reproduced short-wavelength output caused by the finite thickness of the tape''s magnetic coating: at short wavelengths only the layer nearest the head contributes usefully, while deeper magnetization is too far from the head and partially cancels. It is described by the reproduce factor (1 - e^(-2*pi*d/lambda)) / (2*pi*d/lambda), where d is coating thickness and lambda the recorded wavelength, so the loss grows as the coating becomes thick relative to the wavelength.','The magnetic coating on tape has real thickness. For very short, high-pitched wavelengths, only the top layer near the head is heard. The deeper magnetism sits too far away to help and can even cancel out. So thick coatings lose more of the highest frequencies. This effect is called thickness loss.','It explains one of the built-in high-frequency reproduce losses of tape, showing why thinner coatings and shorter recorded wavelengths behave as they do. Understanding it guides tape and speed choices for high-frequency performance.','An engineer treats thickness loss as a fundamental high-frequency limit and reduces its effect with higher tape speed (longer wavelengths) or a suitable tape, rather than only boosting equalization.','Reproduce Losses',ARRAY['Spacing loss','Gap loss','Wavelength','Switching-field distribution','Bias','Equalization']::text[],ARRAY['Confusing thickness loss with spacing loss; spacing loss comes from the distance between tape and head, thickness loss from the coating''s own depth.','Trying to fully recover thickness loss with high-frequency equalization, which also lifts noise and can saturate the tape.','Assuming a thicker coating always sounds better; it raises long-wavelength output but worsens short-wavelength thickness loss.']::text[],ARRAY['Explaining why higher tape speed improves high-frequency response.','Comparing why short wavelengths reproduce weaker than long ones.','Setting reproduce equalization with the tape''s inherent thickness loss in mind.']::text[],'e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM glossary g WHERE g.id='c8309acc-2d56-5fa1-9cce-c72152f1f431' OR regexp_replace(lower(g.term),'[^a-z0-9]','','g')=regexp_replace(lower('Thickness loss'),'[^a-z0-9]','','g'));
INSERT INTO glossary_topics (glossary_id,achievement_id,is_primary)
SELECT 'c8309acc-2d56-5fa1-9cce-c72152f1f431','e9c64f09-41cc-480b-affc-4e0919253e5c',true
